Native Amiga audio.device DMA regression
=======================================

Run audioflush on native Amiga hardware or an emulator implementing Paula.
The normal and quick audio device test groups select it only for Amiga m68k.

A silent, repeating Chip RAM sample keeps channel 0 busy. CMD_FLUSH must
disable its DMA and abort both the active request and any pending write.
Aborting only a pending write must leave the current sample's DMA running.
The test checks DMACONR and I/O completion, independently of driver internals.
It explicitly disables channel DMA before freeing sample memory, including
when the unchanged driver fails. No audible sound or copyrighted data needed.

Build: test-devices-audio or test-devices-audio-quick
